rachael: Heads-up: hit a confusing UX in the Go generator stream. If an auth refresh triggers mid-stream and the app cancels the parent ctx the SDK sometimes leaves a reader goroutine alive. Minimal repro added to sampleapp/generator/ctx-signal-issue.go. Anyone seen this?

omar: Yep. Root cause is mixing user ctx cancellation with transport lifecycle. Internal reconnect tries to push to outCh while cancel raced; if you close outCh from two places you get a blocked send.

patrick: Short term: internal transport should use its own context and an `abandoned` atomic flag. If auth fails set abandoned=true and cancel transportCtx; don't close user-facing channels from transport code.

rachael: quick sketch I used to fix locally:
```go
transportCtx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
var abandoned atomic.Bool
// reader loop
for {
  select {
  case <-transportCtx.Done(): return
  case ev := <-recv:
    if abandoned.Load() { continue }
    out <- ev
  }
}
// on auth expiry
abandoned.Store(true)
cancel()
```

omar: Agree. Long term: add explicit Abort() on GeneratorStream so apps can indicate intent without canceling their ctx. Resume(token) can be added later with resume_token semantics.

patrick: CI note: saw generator_stream_race_test flake. Filed https://ci/internal/4621. I'll pin the test to flaky until we add the Abort API. ci-bot: :robot_face: nightly flaky: 2/10.

rachael: Action items: patrick -> small patch to use transportCtx + abandoned flag; omar -> open design doc for Abort() semantics; I'll update sampleapp/generator with a pattern showing Abort vs ctx cancel. Thanks. :thumbsup:
